🌍 Terraform
Definition:
Terraform is an Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tool used to automate infrastructure creation and management on any cloud platform.
Infrastructure:- Resources used to run our application.
---
🧠 Basic Information
Used to automate infrastructure setup (like EC2, VPC, S3, etc.)
Manual setup is time-consuming and prone to errors — Terraform solves this.
Written in Go language
Uses HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language)
Free & Open-Source
Platform Independent (works on any OS)
Released in 2014
Invented by: Mitchell Hashimoto
Owned by: HashiCorp Company
Terraform can also be called a IaC (Infrastructure as Code) tool.
---
[CONFIG] Why Terraform
Before Terraform, every cloud had its own automation tool:
AWS → CloudFormation
Azure → ARM Templates
GCP → Deployment Manager
Terraform unified this — one tool for all cloud platforms (AWS, Azure, GCP, etc.)
---
🌟 Advantages
Reusable: Same code can be used across environments
Time-Saving: Deploy entire infra in seconds
Automation: Reduces manual work
Avoids Mistakes: Consistent and repeatable deployments
Dry Run (Plan): Preview changes before applying
Multi-Cloud: Supports multiple providers
Version Controlled: Works with Git for collaboration
🧩 Summary
> Terraform automates infrastructure creation using code, saving time, avoiding manual errors, and supporting multiple cloud providers — all from a single tool.
🏗️ Terraform Architecture & Workflow
🧠 Main Components
1. Configuration Files (.tf)
Written in HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language)
Define your infrastructure (VPCs, EC2s, S3, etc.)
Example:
resource "aws_instance" "example" {
ami           = "ami-0abcd1234"
instance_type = "t2.micro"
}
2. Provider
Plugin that connects Terraform to the actual platform (AWS, Azure, etc.)
Example:
provider "aws" {
region = "us-east-1"
}
3. Resources
Real cloud components (EC2, S3, VPC, etc.) created by Terraform.
Defined inside configuration files.
4. State File (terraform.tfstate)
Keeps track of your created resources.
Acts as a “map” of what’s deployed.
If deleted → Terraform won’t know existing infra.
5. Terraform Core
The main engine that reads configuration, interacts with providers, and manages state.
---
[CONFIG] Terraform Workflow
1. Write – Create .tf files with resources.
2. Init – Run terraform init
Downloads provider plugins (like AWS).
3. Plan – Run terraform plan
Shows what Terraform will create or change (dry run).
4. Apply – Run terraform apply
Actually creates the infrastructure.
5. Destroy – Run terraform destroy
Deletes everything defined in the .tf files.
---
🧩 Flow Diagram (Text View)
Write Code (.tf)
↓
terraform init
↓
terraform plan
↓
terraform apply
↓
Infrastructure Created
↓
terraform destroy (optional)
---
[INFO] Summary
> Terraform follows a simple flow — write, plan, apply — using configuration files and providers. It keeps track of your infra with the state file and can deploy or destroy entire environments automatically.
