🔹 1. BASIC WORKFLOW COMMANDS
1️⃣ terraform init
Purpose:
Initializes your Terraform working directory.
What it does:
Downloads provider plugins (e.g., AWS, Azure).
Prepares the backend (state storage).
Sets up the environment for Terraform commands.
Example:
terraform init
✔ Run this once in every new project before plan/apply.
---
2️⃣ terraform validate
Purpose:
Checks your .tf files for syntax errors.
Example:
terraform validate
✔ Helps ensure your configuration is valid before planning.
---
3️⃣ terraform plan
Purpose:
Shows a preview of what Terraform will create, change, or destroy.
Example:
terraform plan
✔ Think of this as a dry run — no real changes yet.
To save the plan:
terraform plan -out=plan.tfplan
---
4️⃣ terraform apply
Purpose:
Actually applies the configuration and creates or updates infrastructure.
Example:
terraform apply
Or apply from a saved plan:
terraform apply plan.tfplan
✔ This command creates all resources defined in .tf files.
---
5️⃣ terraform destroy
Purpose:
Deletes everything that Terraform created.
Example:
terraform destroy
✔ Use this to clean up your AWS account or test environments.
---
6️⃣ terraform show
Purpose:
Displays details about your current infrastructure and state.
Example:
terraform show
---
7️⃣ terraform output
Purpose:
Displays all output values defined in your configuration (from outputs.tf).
Example:
terraform output
To see a specific output:
terraform output ec2_public_ip
---
🔹 2. STATE MANAGEMENT COMMANDS
Terraform tracks all resources in a state file (terraform.tfstate).
Here are commands to manage it:
1️⃣ terraform state list
Lists all resources tracked in the state file.
terraform state list
2️⃣ terraform state show <resource>
Shows details about a specific resource from the state.
terraform state show aws_instance.myec2
3️⃣ terraform state rm <resource>
Removes a resource from state file (without deleting in cloud).
terraform state rm aws_instance.myec2
4️⃣ terraform state mv <source> <destination>
Moves resources between modules or renames them in state.
terraform state mv aws_instance.old aws_instance.new
---
🔹 3. WORKSPACE COMMANDS
Workspaces let you manage multiple environments (like dev, test, prod)
with the same Terraform configuration.
1️⃣ terraform workspace list
Lists all available workspaces.
2️⃣ terraform workspace new <name>
Creates a new workspace.
terraform workspace new dev
3️⃣ terraform workspace select <name>
Switches to another workspace.
terraform workspace select prod
4️⃣ terraform workspace show
Displays the current workspace.
5.terraform workspace delete
---
🔹 4. FORMATTING AND TIDY-UP COMMANDS
1️⃣ terraform fmt
Formats all .tf files to a consistent style.
terraform fmt
2️⃣ terraform fmt -recursive
Formats all Terraform files in subfolders too.
---
🔹 5. LOCKING AND VERSION CHECK COMMANDS
1️⃣ terraform version
Shows the current Terraform version.
terraform version
2️⃣ terraform providers
Shows all providers used in your configuration.
terraform providers
3️⃣ terraform providers lock
Locks provider versions to avoid mismatches.
---
terraform taint
Purpose: Marks a resource as tainted (damaged) so Terraform will destroy and recreate it on the next apply.
Syntax:- terraform taint aws_instance.one.id
terraform untaint
Purpose: Removes the tainted status from a resource so it won’t be recreated.
Syntax:- terraform untaint aws_instance.one.id
Use Case: If you mistakenly tainted a resource or decide it doesn’t need replacement.
[developer....hicorp.com]
🔹 6. IMPORTING EXISTING RESOURCES
terraform import
Brings existing cloud resources under Terraform management.
Example:
terraform import aws_instance.example i-0abcd1234
