🧩 Terraform Modules, Backends & Remote State
🧱 1. Terraform Modules
🌍 Definition:
A module in Terraform is a container for multiple resources that work together.
It allows you to reuse, organize, and simplify your code.
You can think of a module as a function in programming —
you define it once, and call it many times with different values.
---
🗂️ Structure of a Terraform Module
A module usually has:
main.tf
variables.tf
outputs.tf
Each module should describe a small logical part of infrastructure (like a VPC, EC2, or S3).
---
🧩 Example – Creating a VPC Module
📁 Folder Structure
terraform-project/
│
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
├── outputs.tf
└── modules/
└── vpc/
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
└── outputs.tf
---
[INFO] Inside modules/vpc/main.tf
resource "aws_vpc" "main" {
cidr_block = var.cidr
tags = {
Name = var.vpc_name
}
}
[INFO] Inside modules/vpc/variables.tf
variable "cidr" {
description = "VPC CIDR block"
type        = string
}
variable "vpc_name" {
description = "VPC name tag"
type        = string
}
[INFO] Inside modules/vpc/outputs.tf
output "vpc_id" {
value = aws_vpc.main.id
}
---
🧩 Now call the module from your root main.tf
module "vpc" {
source   = "./modules/vpc"
cidr     = "10.0.0.0/16"
vpc_name = "demo-vpc"
}
✔ When you run terraform apply, Terraform loads the module and creates the VPC.
---
🌟 Advantages of Using Modules
Advantage	Description
Reusability	Write once, use anywhere
Simplifies Code	Keeps main project clean
Team Collaboration	Everyone can use same modules
Consistency	Same infra standards across environments
Versioning	You can version your modules using Git or Terraform Registry
---
🧠 Example — Using Terraform Public Module
module "vpc" {
source  = "terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws"
version = "5.1.0"
name = "my-vpc"
cidr = "10.0.0.0/16"
azs  = ["us-east-1a", "us-east-1b"]
public_subnets = ["10.0.1.0/24", "10.0.2.0/24"]
private_subnets = ["10.0.3.0/24", "10.0.4.0/24"]
}
✔ Terraform downloads it automatically from the Terraform Registry.
---
💾 2. Terraform Backends
---
🌍 Definition:
A backend in Terraform defines where and how the state file is stored.
By default, Terraform keeps your state file locally (in terraform.tfstate).
But for team environments, you store it remotely (like in S3, Azure Blob, GCS, etc.).
---
[INFO] Example — Local Backend (default)
terraform {
backend "local" {
path = "terraform.tfstate"
}
}
---
[INFO] Example — Remote Backend (AWS S3)
terraform {
backend "s3" {
bucket         = "my-terraform-state-bucket"
key            = "env/dev/terraform.tfstate"
region         = "us-east-1"
dynamodb_table = "terraform-lock"
encrypt        = true
}
}
---
🧠 Explanation:
Parameter	Description
bucket	Name of the S3 bucket storing the state file
key	Path inside the bucket where the state file is saved
region	AWS region of the bucket
dynamodb_table	Optional table for state locking (prevents 2 users editing same state)
encrypt	Encrypts the state file
---
🌟 Why Use Remote Backend?
Advantage	Description
Team Collaboration	Everyone uses same remote state
State Locking	Avoids concurrent modification
Security	Stored in secure cloud storage
Backups	S3 versioning keeps old state versions
Automation	Ideal for CI/CD pipelines
---
🌐 3. Terraform Remote State
---
[INFO] Definition:
Remote State allows you to share Terraform state data between configurations or teams.
You can output data from one Terraform project and use it in another.
---
🧩 Example – Sharing VPC Info Between Projects
Project 1: VPC
output "vpc_id" {
value = aws_vpc.main.id
}
Project 2: EC2
data "terraform_remote_state" "vpc" {
backend = "s3"
config = {
bucket = "my-terraform-state-bucket"
key    = "env/dev/terraform.tfstate"
region = "us-east-1"
}
}
resource "aws_instance" "example" {
ami           = "ami-0abcd1234"
instance_type = "t2.micro"
subnet_id     = data.terraform_remote_state.vpc.outputs.vpc_id
}
✔ Now your EC2 project automatically fetches the vpc_id from the first project.
---
[CONFIG] 4. Terraform Workspaces
Workspaces are used to manage multiple environments (like dev, test, prod) using the same configuration.
---
🧩 Example Commands
terraform workspace list        # Show all workspaces
terraform workspace new dev     # Create a new workspace
terraform workspace select dev  # Switch to workspace
terraform workspace show        # Display current workspace
Each workspace has its own state file, so resources don’t conflict.
---
🧠 Summary
Concept	Purpose
Modules	Reuse and organize Terraform code
Backend	Where Terraform stores its state (local or remote)
Remote State	Share data between different Terraform projects
Workspaces	Manage multiple environments (dev/test/prod)
---
🚀 Quick Real-World Flow
1. Create modules for VPC, EC2, and S3.
2. Store state file in S3 backend (enable DynamoDB lock).
3. Use remote state to connect EC2 with VPC outputs.
4. Manage environments using workspaces.
5. Use terraform plan and apply to deploy infrastructure.
