[CONFIG] Terraform Provisioners, Data Sources & Meta-Arguments
⚡ 1. Terraform Provisioners
🌍 Definition:
Provisioners are used to execute scripts or commands on a resource after it’s created.
They help in configuring servers — for example:
Installing software packages
Updating system settings
Running shell or Ansible scripts
---
🧩 Types of Provisioners
Type	Description
local-exec	Runs a command on your local machine (where Terraform is running)
remote-exec	Runs a command inside the created resource (like EC2 via SSH)
file	Uploads files from local machine to remote server
---
🧱 Example 1 — Local Exec Provisioner
resource "aws_instance" "example" {
ami           = "ami-0abcd1234"
instance_type = "t2.micro"
provisioner "local-exec" {
command = "echo EC2 instance created successfully!"
}
}
✔ Runs this command on your local system after instance creation.
---
🧱 Example 2 — Remote Exec Provisioner
resource "aws_instance" "example" {
ami           = "ami-0abcd1234"
instance_type = "t2.micro"
key_name      = "mykey"
connection {
type        = "ssh"
user        = "ec2-user"
private_key = file("mykey.pem")
host        = self.public_ip
}
provisioner "remote-exec" {
inline = [
"sudo yum update -y",
"sudo yum install nginx -y",
"sudo systemctl start nginx"
]
}
}
✔ Runs commands directly inside the EC2 instance after it’s created.
---
🧱 Example 3 — File Provisioner
provisioner "file" {
source      = "index.html"
destination = "/var/www/html/index.html"
}
✔ Copies local file → EC2 instance.
---
⚠️ Important:
Provisioners should be used only when absolutely necessary —
Terraform prefers infrastructure creation, not configuration.
For configuration, tools like Ansible are better.
---
🔍 2. Terraform Data Sources
---
🌍 Definition:
Data Sources let you fetch existing information from the cloud provider —
instead of creating new resources.
Useful when you need details like:
Existing VPC ID
Existing AMI ID
Existing Security Group name
---
🧱 Example 1 — Fetch Latest Amazon Linux AMI
data "aws_ami" "amazon_linux" {
most_recent = true
owners      = ["amazon"]
filter {
name   = "name"
values = ["amzn2-ami-hvm-*-x86_64-gp2"]
}
}
resource "aws_instance" "example" {
ami           = data.aws_ami.amazon_linux.id
instance_type = "t2.micro"
}
✔ This ensures Terraform always picks the latest Amazon Linux AMI.
---
🧱 Example 2 — Fetch Existing VPC
data "aws_vpc" "default" {
default = true
}
output "vpc_id" {
value = data.aws_vpc.default.id
}
✔ Pulls information about the default VPC instead of creating a new one.
---
🌟 Advantages of Data Sources
Benefit	Description
Dynamic	Always fetches latest data
Reusability	Can be shared between modules
Automation	Avoids hardcoding IDs
Integration	Connects with existing infrastructure
---
🧩 3. Terraform Meta-Arguments
---
Meta-arguments control how Terraform handles resources —
looping, dependency, conditional creation, etc.
---
🔗 (a) depends_on
Defines explicit dependency between resources.
resource "aws_instance" "example" {
ami           = "ami-0abcd1234"
instance_type = "t2.micro"
depends_on    = [aws_vpc.main]
}
✔ Ensures the EC2 instance is created after the VPC.
---
🔁 (b) count
Used to create multiple instances of the same resource.
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
count         = 3
ami           = "ami-0abcd1234"
instance_type = "t2.micro"
}
✔ Creates 3 EC2 instances (web[0], web[1], web[2]).
---
🔄 (c) for_each
Used for looping through maps or sets (more advanced than count).
variable "subnets" {
type = map(string)
default = {
subnet1 = "10.0.1.0/24"
subnet2 = "10.0.2.0/24"
}
}
resource "aws_subnet" "example" {
for_each   = var.subnets
vpc_id     = aws_vpc.main.id
cidr_block = each.value
tags = {
Name = each.key
}
}
✔ Creates two subnets dynamically with unique tags.
---
[CONFIG] (d) lifecycle
Controls how Terraform handles resource changes.
resource "aws_instance" "example" {
ami           = "ami-0abcd1234"
instance_type = "t2.micro"
lifecycle {
prevent_destroy = true
}
}
✔ Prevents accidental deletion of important resources.
---
🧩 Common Lifecycle Arguments
Argument	Description
create_before_destroy	Creates a new resource before destroying the old one
prevent_destroy	Prevents resource deletion
ignore_changes	Ignores specified attributes from being updated
---
🔧 (e) dynamic
Used to generate nested blocks dynamically (for variable structures).
variable "ingress_rules" {
default = [
{ from_port = 22, to_port = 22, protocol = "tcp" },
{ from_port = 80, to_port = 80, protocol = "tcp" }
]
}
resource "aws_security_group" "example" {
name = "demo-sg"
dynamic "ingress" {
for_each = var.ingress_rules
content {
from_port   = ingress.value.from_port
to_port     = ingress.value.to_port
protocol    = ingress.value.protocol
cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
}
}
}
✔ Automatically generates ingress rules from variable input.
---
🧠 Summary Table
Concept	Purpose	Example Use
Provisioners	Run commands/scripts after resource creation	Install nginx on EC2
Data Sources	Fetch existing infra info	Get latest AMI ID
depends_on	Define resource order	EC2 depends on VPC
count / for_each	Create multiple resources dynamically	Multiple subnets or instances
lifecycle	Protect or control resource updates	Prevent destroy
dynamic	Generate nested blocks dynamically	Create SG rules from list
---
🚀 Real Example Flow:
1. Create a VPC (with for_each for multiple subnets).
2. Launch EC2 using latest AMI from data source.
3. Add provisioner to install Nginx automatically.
4. Use lifecycle to prevent EC2 deletion accidentally.
5. Reuse module for multiple environments.
